[Event "Italian opening: the quiet Italian"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/vMvQfAPq/p7x52xz5"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis"]
[Role "teaching"]

{ Today we learn the Italian. Open with the king pawn, two squares, and grab the middle. [%cal Ye2e4] }
1. e4 { Straight into the middle. Centre pawns make room for all your pieces. [%csl Gd5,Gf5] } (1. d4 { Also excellent, and the door into a whole different world. } 1... d5 2. c4 { That is the queens gambit. A fine opening, just not today. }) (1. Nf3 { A good move too. It reaches the same squares, only slower. } 1... d5 2. d4 { Sound chess. We want our bishop out fast. }) 1... e5 { He copies you. Now a knight comes out and leans on that pawn. [%cal Yg1f3] } 2. Nf3 { Knights before bishops, and this one already attacks e5. [%cal Gf3e5] } (2. Bc4 { The bishop is happy there, and we play it next move anyway. } 2... Nf6 3. Nf3 { The same house by another door. Knights usually go first. }) (2. Qh5 { Never send the queen out early. She is big, and everyone chases her. } 2... Nc6 3. Bc4 g6 { His pawn kicks her away and he develops for free. }) (2. f4 { The kings gambit. Wild, famous, and a lesson for another day. } 2... exf4 { He is a pawn up and the game turns crazy. }) (2. d4 { The centre game. He simply takes, and you must go and win it back. } 2... exd4 3. Nf3 { Playable. We prefer to keep our pawn on e4. }) 2... Nc6 { He guards it. Your bishop wants the square that stares at f7. [%cal Yf1c4] } 3. Bc4 { The Italian. That bishop points straight at his weakest square. [%csl Rf7][%cal Gc4f7] } (3. Bb5 { The Spanish, and just as strong. But it is a much longer story. } 3... a6 4. Ba4 { Excellent chess. We want the quick aim at f7. }) (3. d4 { The Scotch. Also good, and another opening to learn. } 3... exd4 4. Nxd4 { Fine for you. Today the bishop goes hunting instead. }) 3... Bc5 { He copies again. Build a little wall with the c pawn. [%cal Yc2c3] } 4. c3 { Quiet, with a big idea behind it: soon your pawn goes to d4 and takes the whole centre. [%csl Gd4] } (4. Bxf7+ { Greedy. Check first, think later, and it costs a whole bishop. } 4... Kxf7 5. Nxe5+ Nxe5 { A bishop for one pawn, and his king is perfectly fine. }) (4. Ng5 { Too soon. Nothing out there is guarding your knight. } 4... Qxg5 { His queen simply eats it. Wait until f7 is really weak. }) (4. d3 { Completely fine. The same set up in another order. } 4... Nf6 5. c3 { We land in the same place. }) (4. b4 { The Evans gambit. A wild pawn offer, and great fun another day. } 4... Bxb4 5. c3 { Sharp stuff. Today we stay calm. }) (4. O-O { Safe and good. Castling early is never really wrong. } 4... Nf6 5. c3 { The same moves in another order. }) 4... Nf6 { His knight hits your e pawn, so d4 has to wait. Prop that pawn up with a small step. [%cal Yd2d3] } 5. d3 { It guards e4 and frees your dark bishop. The push to d4 comes a little later. [%csl Ge4,Gd4] } (5. O-O { Castling is good, but he can snap your e pawn right now. } 5... Nxe4 { Free food. The little pawn to d3 stops all of that. }) (5. d4 { The big centre. A real line, and much sharper than ours. } 5... exd4 6. e5 { Exciting, and an awful lot to remember. }) (5. Ng5 { Still too early. His king simply runs to safety. } 5... O-O { Your knight has nothing to hit and must come home. }) 5... d6 { He does the same. Everyone is out on that side, so get the king safe. [%cal Ye1g1] } 6. O-O { King tucked away, rook joins in. Castle early and sleep well. [%csl Gg1] } (6. Bb3 { Also fine: tuck the bishop away first. } 6... O-O 7. O-O { The same safe position, one move later. }) (6. Nbd2 { The knight heads to d2 right away. Also fine — we get there anyway. } 6... O-O 7. O-O { The same plan, just a different move order. }) (6. Bg5 { A pin, but there is nothing behind that knight yet. } 6... h6 7. Bh4 g5 { He chases your bishop and grabs space. }) (6. h3 { A useful little move, and quite playable. } 6... O-O 7. O-O { You castle anyway, one move later. }) (6. Ng5 { The third time is not the charm. } 6... O-O 7. Nf3 { Out and back. Two moves for nothing. }) 6... O-O { Both kings are home. One piece is still fast asleep. [%cal Yb1d2] } 7. Nbd2 { That last knight joins the party on d2. Both kings are tucked away, and your pawns stand shoulder to shoulder in the centre. } (7. Bb3 { Just as good: park the bishop safely before anything else. } 7... a6 8. Nbd2 { The same plan, a different move order. }) (7. h3 { Also fine, and very normal. } 7... a6 8. Nbd2 { The knight comes anyway. }) (7. Re1 { Good too. The rook belongs on that column. } 7... a6 8. Nbd2 { The same plan in another order. }) (7. Ng5 { Once more, and once more it comes straight back. } 7... h6 8. Nf3 { Nothing gained at all. }) 1-0


[Event "Italian opening: the fried liver"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/vMvQfAPq/1VLehfWQ"]
[FEN "r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/2B1P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 3 3"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/2B1P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQK2R_b_KQkq_-_3_3"]
[Role "teaching"]

{ Two pieces are out on each side, and it is his move. Watch which way his knight goes. }
3... Nf6 { This time his knight goes to f6. Now for some mischief: jump the knight to g5. [%cal Yf3g5] } 4. Ng5 { Two pieces now stare at f7, and only his king is guarding it. [%cal Gg5f7,Gc4f7] } (4. d3 { Solid, safe and sleepy. There is something much sharper here. } 4... Bc5 5. c3 { A fine quiet game, and no fried liver. }) (4. O-O { Castling is good, but he grabs your e pawn at once. } 4... Nxe4 { The pawn was loose. The jump to g5 was the moment. }) (4. Bxf7+ { Never just hand the bishop over. } 4... Kxf7 { A bishop for one pawn, and no attack at all. }) (4. d4 { A real move, and a whole different game. } 4... exd4 5. e5 { Playable, but we want f7 today. }) 4... d5 { His best answer. Grabbing on e4 instead loses to a check on f7. Take with the pawn. [%cal Ye4d5] } 5. exd5 { Take with the pawn, so your bishop keeps staring at f7. [%cal Gc4f7] } (5. Bxd5 { The wrong capture. Look at your knight out on g5. } 5... Nxd5 6. exd5 Qxg5 { His queen collects it. A whole piece for nothing. }) (5. Nxf7 { Too early. The pawn on d5 comes first. } 5... Kxf7 6. exd5 { A knight for one pawn, and his king walks home. }) (5. d3 { You forgot the pawn standing on d5. } 5... dxc4 { It just takes your bishop. }) 5... Nxd5 { He takes back with the knight, and that is the mistake — the knight to a5 was the move that kept him safe. Now jump in. [%cal Yg5f7] } 6. Nxf7 { The fried liver. The knight gives itself up to drag his king out. [%cal Gf7d8,Gf7h8] } (6. Bxd5 { You win the knight back and hand him everything else. } 6... Qxd5 7. O-O Bf5 { His queen lands in the middle and you are worse. }) (6. O-O { Castling is fine, but you forgot your own knight. } 6... Qxg5 { She eats it. You take back on d5, and the fried liver has gone. }) (6. d4 { A good move, and a slow one. The jump was the whole idea. } 6... Nxd4 7. c3 { Still fine for you, but the fun has gone. }) 6... Kxf7 { He has to take. Now check at once, before he can breathe. [%cal Yd1f3] } 7. Qf3+ { Check, and your queen hits the knight on d5 as well. [%cal Gf3d5] } (7. Qh5+ { A check, but from the wrong square. } 7... Kg8 { His king steps home and your attack is over. }) (7. Bxd5+ { The pin looks tasty, but his queen takes it back. } 7... Qxd5 { A whole piece for nothing. You are lost. }) (7. Nc3 { A fine developing move, and it gives him a free one. } 7... Be6 { His bishop guards everything and the attack has gone. }) (7. O-O { Far too slow. The check had to come now. } 7... Be6 { He tidies up and you are simply a piece down. }) 7... Ke6 { His king must walk out to guard the knight. Bring the last knight in. [%cal Yb1c3] } 8. Nc3 { It hits the pinned knight, and his king is stuck in the open. [%tail][%csl Re6][%cal Gc3d5] } (8. Bxd5+ { Taking wins the piece back and throws the attack away. } 8... Qxd5 9. Qxd5+ Kxd5 { Queens off, his king is safe, and you are a piece down. }) (8. d4 { Natural, and his knight jumps in on your queen. } 8... Nxd4 { She must run, the attack dies, and he stays a piece up. }) (8. O-O { Castling is much too slow here. } 8... b5 { His pawn hits your bishop and his king escapes. }) 8... Nb4 { He jumps the knight to b4, eyeing c2 and a fork of your king and rook. } 9. O-O { Castle now, and that fork on c2 bites on nothing. Your king is finally safe too. [%csl Gg1] } 9... c6 { A quiet prop for the centre. His king still has nowhere to run. } 10. d4 { The centre cracks open, and his e pawn has only his own king left to protect it. [%csl Gd4] } 10... Qf6 { The queen comes out to help hold everything together. } 11. Qd1 { Your queen steps back to d1, ready to join the party down the centre. } 11... Ke7 { His king shuffles again, still hunting for a corner that does not exist. } 12. Re1 { The last rook joins in, aiming straight up the e file at that pawn — and at his king behind it. [%csl Ge5] } 12... Qg6 { The queen finds a fresh square, eyeing g2 all the way down the file. } 13. Nxd5+ { Check! Trade for trade, and his king still cannot rest. [%csl Ge7] } 13... Nxd5 { He takes back with his other knight — his last spare piece, and still no safety. } 14. Rxe5+ { Check again, and the pawn falls too. You have won back most of what you gave, and his king still has nowhere to hide. [%csl Ge5] } 1-0


[Event "Italian opening: he plays too slowly"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/vMvQfAPq/9JZcZY19"]
[FEN "r1bqk1nr/pppp1ppp/2n5/2b1p3/2B1P3/2P2N2/PP1P1PPP/RNBQK2R b KQkq - 0 4"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqk1nr/pppp1ppp/2n5/2b1p3/2B1P3/2P2N2/PP1P1PPP/RNBQK2R_b_KQkq_-_0_4"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ Same opening again. Now see how he handles a quiet position. }
4... d6 { He answers with a quiet little pawn move. How do you punish a slow move? } 5. d4 { Exactly. Take the whole middle while he is still shuffling. [%csl Gd4,Ge4] } (5. d3 { Fine, but far too polite. He played slowly: take everything. } 5... Nf6 6. O-O { Level chess. The big push was the punishment. }) (5. O-O { Safe and good, and it misses the moment. } 5... Nf6 6. d4 { You want that push anyway. }) (5. Ng5 { Nothing out there guards your knight. } 5... Qxg5 { His queen takes it for free. }) (5. Bxf7+ { A bishop for one pawn is a bad trade. } 5... Kxf7 { His king is safe enough and you are a piece down. }) 5... exd4 { He takes. Which recapture leaves two pawns side by side in the middle? } 6. cxd4 { The pawn takes, and that pair in the centre is gold. [%csl Gd4,Ge4] } (6. Nxd4 { The knight takes, and your lovely centre disappears. } 6... Nf6 7. O-O { Level, and the pawn pair has gone. }) (6. e5 { Too fast. That pawn is not ready to run. } 6... Nxe5 7. Nxe5 dxe5 { He takes twice and you have simply lost a pawn. }) (6. O-O { Castling is never silly, but the pawn was hanging. } 6... dxc3 { He keeps his extra pawn. }) 6... Bb6 { His bishop steps out of the way. Now finish the job. } 7. O-O { A big centre, a safe king, and half his pieces are still at home. } (7. h3 { Also fine, and useful later. } 7... Nf6 8. O-O { You castle anyway. }) (7. Bb5 { A good square too. } 7... Bd7 8. Nc3 { Sound chess, and castling was simpler. }) (7. d5 { Pushing past hands his knight a lovely square. } 7... Ne5 8. Nxe5 dxe5 { Still fine for you, but the centre is stuck. }) 1-0


[Event "Italian opening: the plan after castling"]
[Result "1-0"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/vMvQfAPq/C9QG2PSv"]
[FEN "r1bq1rk1/ppp2ppp/2np1n2/2b1p3/2B1P3/2PP1N2/PP1N1PPP/R1BQ1RK1 b - - 3 7"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bq1rk1/ppp2ppp/2np1n2/2b1p3/2B1P3/2PP1N2/PP1N1PPP/R1BQ1RK1_b_-_-_3_7"]
[Role "teaching"]

{ Both kings are tucked away, and the middlegame begins. }
7... h6 { A little luft, nothing more. So what now? Time to bring a rook into the game. [%cal Yf1e1] } 8. Re1 { The rook lines up behind your e pawn, where the game will open. [%csl Ge4] } (8. d4 { Too early. The break needs your pieces ready first. } 8... exd4 9. Nxd4 Nxd4 { His knights trade off and that pawn will drop. }) (8. h3 { Useful too: it stops his knight coming to g4. } 8... a6 9. Re1 { The rook comes anyway. }) (8. b4 { Space on the other side, and quite playable. } 8... Bb6 9. a4 { Sound chess. We are heading for the king side. }) 8... a6 { A quiet, useful little move. Nothing urgent yet — so the knight begins its trip. } 9. Nf1 { The sleepy knight starts its trip: d2, then f1, and next stop g3. [%cal Gf1g3] } (9. d4 { Still too soon. Send the knight travelling first. } 9... exd4 { He takes, and every way you take back leaves you worse. }) (9. h3 { Also useful. The knight can travel next move. } 9... Be6 { Fine as well. Your knight still wants to reach g3. }) 9... Be6 { His last minor piece comes out too. Every one of his bishops and knights is finally working. } 10. Ng3 { It lands on g3, eyeing f5 and h5. That was the whole plan: every piece now has real work to do. [%csl Gf5,Gh5] } (10. Bxe6 { Trading first is fine too. } 10... fxe6 11. Ng3 { The knight goes anyway, and his pawns are doubled. }) (10. d4 { The break, one move too soon. } 10... Bxc4 11. dxc5 dxc5 { Your good bishop is gone and his pieces are happy. }) 1-0
